.lua
book.chapter(13, "Records and row polymorphism")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Field names make products communicative"
.pp
A record associates labels with values. Its expression syntax uses braces and
field assignments such as name = expression. Projection uses a dot after a
record-valued expression. Records are useful when a fixed product's positions
would be hard to remember: a declaration with name, source, and exported fields
is clearer than a triple whose ordering every caller must learn separately.
Labels are part of the structure, not comments attached to tuple positions.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[person := { name = "Ada", score = 40 }
Test.equal "named field" person.name "Ada"
Test.equal "numeric field" person.score 40
Test.equal "label order" {a = 1, b = 2} {b = 2, a = 1}
describe p := p.name ^ ":" ^ Int.to_string p.score
Test.equal "record consumer" (describe person) "Ada:40"]=],
    "Construction, projection, and structural equality")
.endlua
.pp
The runtime sorts labels for deterministic construction and lookup. Source
field order therefore does not define record identity. This is helpful for
stable rendering, but it does not mean field expressions may be rearranged
without considering their effects. Data layout and evaluation order are
different contracts. Keep construction expressions pure when you want to
treat a record literal as a declarative collection of independent facts.
.sh 2 "Open rows describe minimum requirements"
.pp
A function that only needs a name should not require every caller to use an
identical larger record type. The signature { name : String | r } -> String
states a known field and an open row r for additional fields. It accepts a
record with a score, a record with an address, or a record with both, provided
the name field has the promised type. The extra fields are not discarded by
calling the projection; they simply are not required by that operation.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[get_name :: { name : String | r } -> String
get_name p := p.name
Test.equal "one extension" (get_name {name = "Ada", score = 40}) "Ada"
Test.equal "another extension" (get_name {name = "Grace", active = true}) "Grace"
Test.equal "minimal record" (get_name {name = "Edsger"}) "Edsger"]=],
    "One interface over several record shapes")
.endlua
.pp
Row polymorphism is static. It does not mean a field name can be computed from
an arbitrary string and then looked up without a type-level guarantee.
For string-keyed dynamic storage, use a table or an explicit association list.
The distinction matters in schema design: a record says which named fields
are present, while a dynamic map may need to return Option for every lookup.
Choose according to how much the program knows before runtime.
.sh 2 "Unification aligns labels"
.pp
Ordinary tuple unification pairs components by position. Record unification
must instead align labels, unify the corresponding field types, and account
for labels remaining on either side through row tails. A closed record type
has no unspecified extension, while an open row represents additional
structure. A missing required label or an incompatible field type is a static
problem, not an instruction to return a default value silently.
.lua
book.algorithm("Reason about a record constraint", {
    "Collect the required labels and their types.",
    "Match each required label with the supplied record's label.",
    "Unify the corresponding field types.",
    "Account for unmatched labels through the open row, if present.",
    "Reject missing required fields or incompatible closed shapes.",
    "Preserve the relationship between any shared row variables."
})
.endlua
.pp
The pseudocode is a conceptual guide, not the complete row-unification
algorithm. The implementation handles fresh variables, substitutions, and
recursive constraints. For users, the practical lesson is to write the
smallest honest field requirement. Overly closed signatures force callers
to rebuild equivalent data unnecessarily, while an overly vague dynamic
representation loses guarantees that the checker could have provided.
.sh 2 "Immutable updates and reconstruction"
.pp
Records are immutable values. Updating a logical entity means constructing
another record, either with the parser's record-with form or by explicit
construction. The latter is especially clear when only a few fields exist
and the example is intended as a public contract. The old binding continues
to denote the old value. A field containing a mutable foreign handle is a
separate case: the record remains unchanged while the referred resource can
change internally.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[original := { name = "Ada", score = 40 }
bump p := { name = p.name, score = p.score + 1 }
updated := bump original
Test.equal "new value" updated.score 41
Test.equal "old value" original.score 40
Test.equal "copied identity" updated.name original.name]=], "Explicit immutable reconstruction")
.endlua
.pp
Explicit reconstruction also defines what happens to extra fields: this bump
returns exactly the fields it constructs. Do not describe it as preserving
an arbitrary open row merely because its input projections are flexible.
Input extensibility and output preservation are distinct properties. When
designing a generic record transformation, make both properties visible in
its type and tests, including a caller with an extra field that matters.
.sh 2 "Patterns and domain equality"
.pp
Record patterns unpack named fields, and an open pattern can use .. for
additional fields. They are useful when several fields are needed together,
but a simple projection function often gives a clearer reusable interface.
Dot syntax also serves module paths, so maintain conventional capitalization
to distinguish List.map from value.name. An unfamiliar dotted expression is
a good reason to inspect its inferred type rather than guess its namespace.
.pp
Structural record equality includes labels and values. Adding a function-valued
field can make a previously comparable record unsuitable for generic equality.
For domain entities, compare a stable identifier or selected semantic fields
instead of treating every implementation detail as part of identity. A
compiler's source-location annotation, for example, may matter for diagnostics
but not for an optimization's notion of equivalent expressions.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Inspect record expression and pattern handling, make_record, record_field,
and row unification in both runtimes. Model a compiler declaration with name,
arity, and source fields, then write a row-polymorphic name projection and a
specific validation function. Add an extra field at one call site and observe
which functions still apply. Finally, explain why a transformation that
reconstructs only name and arity should not claim to preserve the caller's
source annotation.
